linkup.c and linkup.h are original darwin-lab code, written as a rewrite of
hardware steps. No source text, comment or table from any other project is
copied into them or into their tests.

Hardware facts (register offsets, bit positions, step order, poll intervals and
limits, the RID-to-SID entry layout) were learned by reading, as a hardware map
only, the Linux Apple PCIe host driver:

  sources/linux-aurora-platform-reference (commit bf62c0673ce64c48503739fadf0140db39796e8d)
    drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
    sha256 3ed383956928941e16ec75445c1a94bfbd2f957ccb29634ce88327472701fd37
    licence GPL-2.0, as stated in its own header

That file is not included, quoted or redistributed here. test_linkup.py
--linux-ref checks that no macro or function name defined in it, and no run of
six or more words from its comments, appears in the slice-3 files.

Board values come from the saved J293 Apple Device Tree (private capture, not
in Git): pci-bridge0 function-perst, t-refclk-to-perst, perst-to-config,
#msi-vectors and msi-vector-base; apcie msi-address. The RID-to-SID probe value
is our own. Slice-2 provenance (m1n1, MIT) is in NOTICE-bringup.
